  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["D2"],
      "pins": ["D2"],
      "nets": ["NET0006", "GND"],
      "observed": "On sheet /, D2 is drawn with a diode symbol (triangle + cathode bar) and the reference prefix D2, but its value field reads '1K'. It is connected between NET0006 and GND, while R6 (10K) pulls NET0006 up to +5V. NET0006 is also the MCU pin PB4 (U1.3 / U2.2).",
      "expected": "A two-terminal part's reference prefix, symbol and value must agree: a diode should carry a part number / voltage rating, not a resistance, and a '1K' value implies a resistor (R-prefix, rectangular symbol). As drawn, a diode to GND on a 10K-pulled-up signal net clamps that net to ~0.7 V, so PB4 cannot carry a valid high/tach signal.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "D2 symbol = diode, value = '1K', between NET0006 and GND (sheet /, row B/C)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R6 10K between +5V and NET0006; U1 pin 3 = XTAL2/PB4 = NET0006, U2 pin 2 = PB4 = NET0006"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the specification is silent on the fan controller's internals ('an ATtiny-driven fan controller'), so the part must be judged against standard practice and its own metadata"}
      ],
      "impact": "PB4 (NET0006) is held at a fixed low voltage by the diode clamp (or, if a 1K resistor was intended, mis-biased by the 10K/1K divider), so any function on PB4 (e.g. tachometer input or a status line) reads a constant value; the diode-with-resistance-value mismatch also indicates an assembly/sourcing or editing error.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Apart from this, the drawing is a coherent ATtiny fan controller (alternative THT/SMD MCU footprints, high-side PMOS fan-power switching driven from PB1, potentiometer speed set on PB3, ISP and I2C headers, decoupling). The one defect the drawing supports is D2: a diode symbol/reference carrying the resistor value '1K' on the pulled-up MCU pin NET0006, a component-identity mismatch that also clamps that pin."
}

